{
 "why": "THE MEASUREMENT: every own name an insecure origin takes off the window, and nothing else. Read on 2026-09-19 (PARITY FP5.9) off Chrome 150.0.7871.46 (Chrome's column re-read 2026-10-16 off Chrome 152.0.7977.82, headed on the Wayland desktop with no DevTools client: the same 243 names but EyeDropper, which that Chrome does not expose at all), Brave 151.1.93.138, Edge 151.0.4129.93 and Opera 135.0.5973.133, headed under Xvfb over CDP with --remote-debugging-port=<a free port> (never =0, which sets navigator.webdriver; it read false in all eight runs). ONE python3 http.server, TWO hostnames, so trustworthiness is the only variable: http://127.0.0.1:<p>/page.html (isSecureContext true -- loopback is potentially-trustworthy) and http://web-platform.test:<p>/page.html (isSecureContext false -- it resolves to loopback and is not `localhost`). Object.getOwnPropertyNames(window) at each. A name is IN when a browser that has it on the secure origin does not have it on the insecure one; it is OUT when it survives. The four browsers NEVER disagree about a name they both have (248 names, 229 of them common to all four), which is why this is one platform fact and not a bundle field: `browsers` records which of the four had the name at all. Nothing was gained on the insecure origin, on any browser. WHY NOT THE IDL: webref's [SecureContext] is measurably wrong in BOTH directions here -- it marks 13 interfaces the browsers KEEP (AudioWorkletNode, GeolocationPosition, PushManager, SpeechRecognition, Navigator, Window, ...) and is silent about 40 names they REMOVE (the 26 XR layer/hand/plane interfaces, Worklet, FontData, KeyboardLayoutMap, caches, cookieStore, queryLocalFonts, the three file pickers, the four on* handlers, ...). The spec is a hypothesis; the browser is the authority. WHAT PUTS AN ENTRY IN: re-run the reading above on a newer build and add what it removes. WHAT TAKES ONE OUT: the same reading showing the name survives an insecure origin. Do not add a name because an IDL says [SecureContext], and do not remove one because an IDL is silent.",
 "measured": "2026-09-19",
